/**
 * @mainpage Sushi Slash
 *
 * @section overview Overview
 *
 * Sushi Slash is an LCOM action arcade game inspired by Fruit Ninja. Sushi
 * pieces are launched across the screen and the player cuts them with fast
 * mouse slashes before they fall away. The game tracks score, lives, elapsed
 * session time, current real-world time, and progressively increases pressure
 * through wave and difficulty scheduling.
 *
 * @section gameplay Core Gameplay
 *
 * - Regular sushi awards score when sliced and costs a life when missed.
 * - Bombs are hazards: slicing one ends the current run.
 * - Cut sushi pieces split into independent fragments with their own physics.
 * - Waves control when sushi and hazards appear.
 * - Difficulty scales from EASY to HARD as the player score increases.
 * - Quick slash combos award one extra burst of points after the combo window.
 * - Boss Sushi appears on score milestones and is defeated by typing the
 *   displayed key sequence before its timers expire.
 *
 * @section devices Device Responsibilities
 *
 * - Graphics Card: renders the background, sprites, fragments, menus, HUD, and
 *   mouse slash effects.
 * - Mouse: controls the cursor and creates slash gestures used for hit
 *   detection.
 * - Keyboard: handles seed input and Boss Sushi key sequences.
 * - Timer: drives the game loop, physics updates, animations, waves, and
 *   session timing.
 * - RTC: provides the real-world clock shown in the UI and contributes to
 *   automatic seed generation.
 * - Serial Port: reserved for future multiplayer score or input exchange.
 *
 * @section architecture Project Structure
 *
 * The public API is organized under @c proj/include:
 *
 * - @c game/: gameplay state, sushi objects, waves, menus, boss/combo data,
 *   the application game loop, sprites, and queues.
 * - @c ui/: drawing helpers, screen-specific menu drawing, HUD/status bar,
 *   mouse trail rendering, and the session timer.
 * - @c macros/: shared constants for screen geometry, physics, menus, and
 *   mouse slash tuning.
 * - @c font.h and @c init.h: bitmap font and shared asset initialization APIs.
 *
 * @section flow Runtime Flow
 *
 * proj_main_loop() initializes page-flipped graphics and then calls
 * game_loop(). The loop subscribes to keyboard, timer, and mouse interrupts,
 * dispatches each event to the handler for the active GameState, and releases
 * loop-owned resources when the application exits.
 *
 * A normal run starts in the main menu. When singleplayer begins,
 * gameplay_start() initializes the session data, deterministic seeds, and wave
 * runtime. Timer events call gameplay_update() to advance waves, item physics,
 * missed-item handling, fragments, boss movement, combo timing, and difficulty.
 * Mouse events call gameplay_handle_cut() to process slash collisions. The
 * timer handler then draws the current frame through gameplay_draw(), HUD, and
 * screen-specific UI helpers.
 *
 * On game over, the final screen shows score, maximum combo, sushi cut, bombs
 * avoided, time survived, sushi missed, and the seed used for the run. The
 * player can return to the menu or restart with the same seed.
 *
 * @section docs Documentation Guide
 *
 * Start with game_loop.h for the application loop, game.h for the main state
 * container and gameplay lifecycle, sushi.h for flying objects and cut
 * fragments, waves.h for spawn scheduling, boss.h and combo.h for Boss Sushi,
 * slash_combo.h for quick-slash scoring, game_rand.h for deterministic random
 * generation, and ui.h for rendering helpers. Menu-specific state and drawing
 * APIs live in menu_state.h, pause_menu_state.h, game_over.h, menu_draw.h,
 * pause_menu_draw.h, and game_over_draw.h.
 */
